  "hygieneTssTitle": "Toxic shock syndrome",
  "hygieneTssUrgent": "If you have a sudden high fever, a sunburn-like rash, vomiting, diarrhoea, muscle aches, dizziness or confusion while using a tampon or a menstrual cup: remove it and get urgent medical help.",
  "@hygieneTssUrgent": {"description": "PINNED — hygiene_copy_test.dart asserts this exactly. It describes symptoms and says what to do. It must never name a diagnosis, and must never be softened into a suggestion."},
  "hygieneTssBody": "Toxic shock syndrome is rare and life-threatening. It is caused by bacterial toxins, and it is associated with tampon and menstrual cup use, though it also happens to people who are not menstruating at all.",
  "hygieneTamponsTitle": "Tampons",
  "hygieneTamponsBody": "Regulated as medical devices rather than as drugs. Absorbency ratings are standardised, so the same rating means the same thing across brands. General guidance is to use the lowest absorbency that suits the flow, and to change it as often as the packet says rather than leaving it longer.",
  "hygieneCupsTitle": "Menstrual cups",
  "hygieneCupsBody": "Reusable devices worn internally to collect fluid rather than absorb it. Cases of toxic shock syndrome linked to cup use have been reported. How long a cup can be worn, and how it should be cleaned between uses, genuinely differ between products, so follow the instructions that came with yours rather than a number from an app.",
  "hygieneExternalTitle": "Pads and period underwear",
  "hygieneExternalBody": "Worn outside the body, and not associated with toxic shock syndrome in the way internally worn products are.",
  "nutritionTitle": "Iron and magnesium",
  "nutritionIntro": "Reference notes on two nutrients that come up around menstrual cycles. This is background reading, not advice about your body, and nothing here is based on what you have logged.",
  "@nutritionIntro": {"description": "The framing sentence for the whole screen. It has to establish that the page is reference text and not addressed to the reader personally, before any nutrient is named."},
  "nutritionIronTitle": "Iron",
  "nutritionIronBody": "Iron is part of haemoglobin, the protein that carries oxygen around the blood. Menstrual bleeding is one of the ways the body loses iron, and heavier bleeding means more of it. Heavy menstrual bleeding is a recognised cause of iron deficiency anaemia, whose commonly listed symptoms include tiredness and lack of energy.",
  "nutritionIronDiagnosisNote": "Iron deficiency is confirmed by a blood test. Symptoms alone cannot establish it, and neither can anything in this app.",
  "@nutritionIronDiagnosisNote": {"description": "Sits directly under the iron text. The claim that stops the section reading as a checklist the reader can apply to themselves."},
  "nutritionIronSourcesBody": "Dietary iron comes in two forms: haem iron from meat and fish, and non-haem iron from plants and fortified foods. They are absorbed differently.",
  "nutritionVitaminCTitle": "Why vitamin C is listed here",
  "nutritionVitaminCBody": "Vitamin C eaten in the same meal increases how much non-haem iron the body absorbs. That is the only reason it appears in a menstrual cycle app.",
  "nutritionIronCautionBody": "Iron supplements taken without a confirmed deficiency can do harm, because excess iron accumulates rather than being cleared.",
  "nutritionMagnesiumTitle": "Magnesium",
  "nutritionMagnesiumBody": "Magnesium is involved in muscle and nerve function, among many other processes. It is found in green leafy vegetables, legumes, nuts, seeds and wholegrains.",
  "nutritionMagnesiumEvidenceBody": "Evidence that magnesium supplements improve menstrual symptoms is limited and not conclusive.",
  "@nutritionMagnesiumEvidenceBody": {"description": "Must not be dropped for brevity. It is the claim that stops the magnesium section reading as a recommendation."},
  "nutritionZincTitle": "Why zinc is listed here",
  "nutritionZincBody": "Zinc is an essential mineral involved in immune function and wound healing. It is often sold together with magnesium, which is worth knowing alongside the next line: taken at high doses over time, zinc can interfere with how the body absorbs copper.",
  "nutritionHintTitle": "Something worth mentioning",
  "nutritionHintBody": "On several days recently you logged heavy flow and fatigue together. That pattern is worth raising with a doctor or nurse, who can look into it properly.",
  "@nutritionHintBody": {"description": "PINNED WORDING — nutrition_copy_test.dart asserts this string exactly. It observes two things the user logged and points at a human. It must not name a nutrient, a deficiency, or a supplement, and must not say what the pattern means."},
  "nutritionSourcesNote": "Every claim on this page is listed with its source in NUTRITION_SOURCES.md in the app's source repository.",
  "settingsNutrition": "Iron and magnesium",
  "settingsNutritionSubtitle": "Reference notes on two nutrients that come up around menstrual cycles.",
  "weightTitle": "Body composition and cycle hormones",
  "weightIntro": "Reference notes on how body composition and several hormones interact with the menstrual cycle. This is background reading, not advice, and it describes mechanisms rather than any particular body. Nothing on this page is drawn from anything logged in this app.",
  "@weightIntro": {"description": "The framing sentence for the whole screen. It has to establish that the page is reference text about mechanisms, not addressed to the reader, before anything else is named."},
  "weightNothingToEnterBody": "This app records no body weight and asks for none. There is nothing to enter here, and no figure on this page to measure a body against.",
  "@weightNothingToEnterBody": {"description": "Sits directly under the intro. The section carries a real risk of harm around disordered eating, and the first thing it says is that the app wants nothing from the reader."},
  "weightOestrogenTitle": "Where oestrogen is made",
  "weightOestrogenBody": "Adipose tissue, or body fat, makes the enzyme aromatase, which converts androgens into oestrogens. That reaction happens in the tissue itself, so the ovaries are not the only place oestrogen comes from.",
  "weightOestrogenBalanceBody": "The quantity of adipose tissue is therefore one of the inputs to how much oestrogen is circulating, and it runs in both directions: more of it adds to the total, and very little of it removes a source the ovaries do not make up for. It is one input among several rather than the whole picture.",
  "@weightOestrogenBalanceBody": {"description": "Both ends of the mechanism, deliberately. Stated in one direction only this becomes an argument, and an argument is what this section must never be."},
  "weightEnergyTitle": "Energy availability and the hypothalamus",
  "weightEnergyBody": "The hypothalamus releases GnRH in pulses, and that rhythm is what drives LH and FSH from the pituitary, which in turn drive the ovary. When energy availability is low the pulses are suppressed: LH and FSH fall, ovulation stops, and periods stop with it. That state has a name: functional hypothalamic amenorrhoea.",
  "weightEnergyNotAScaleBody": "Energy availability is what is left of the energy taken in once exercise has had its share, measured against what the body needs to run on. It is not a body weight and it is not a reading from a scale. Functional hypothalamic amenorrhoea occurs in people whose body weight is entirely unremarkable, and heavy training, illness or sustained stress can produce it with no fall in body weight at all.",
  "@weightEnergyNotAScaleBody": {"description": "Load-bearing. Without it the section reads as a statement about a number on a scale, which is both wrong and the most harmful thing this reference could be mistaken for."},
  "weightInsulinTitle": "Insulin, SHBG and free androgen",
  "weightInsulinBody": "Sex hormone binding globulin, or SHBG, is a protein the liver puts into the blood, where it binds testosterone. Bound testosterone is held out of circulation, so only the free fraction reaches tissue. Insulin resistance lowers SHBG, and as SHBG falls the free fraction rises, even when the total amount of testosterone has not changed.",
  "weightPcosTitle": "PCOS",
  "weightPcosBody": "Polycystic ovary syndrome is androgen excess together with irregular or absent ovulation. Insulin resistance is common alongside it, and the mechanism above is part of how the androgen excess is sustained.",
  "weightPcosSpectrumBody": "PCOS occurs across the whole weight spectrum, in people who are thin as well as in people who are not. The widespread impression that it belongs at one end of that spectrum is not accurate, and it is one of the reasons the condition goes unrecognised.",
  "@weightPcosSpectrumBody": {"description": "Must not be dropped. The opposite impression is both common and false, and naming PCOS in a section about body composition without saying this would reinforce the error."},
  "weightThyroidTitle": "Thyroid function",
  "weightThyroidBody": "Thyroid disease reaches the menstrual cycle too, and in both directions. An underactive thyroid is associated with heavier and more frequent bleeding; an overactive one with lighter, less frequent bleeding, or with none. Thyroid function is established by a blood test rather than by the shape of a cycle.",
  "weightChangeTitle": "When a cycle changes",
  "weightChangeBody": "A change in cycle length, in the amount of bleeding, or in whether periods arrive at all has many possible causes, and the mechanisms on this page are only some of them. Cycle dates cannot separate one cause from another, and nothing in this app can diagnose anything. A change worth explaining is worth taking to a doctor or nurse.",
  "@weightChangeBody": {"description": "The last thing on the screen. A page of mechanisms with no exit is an invitation to pick one, so this points at a person. Deliberately carries no threshold: a threshold is a number, and a number here is something to measure a body against."},
  "weightSourcesNote": "Every claim on this page is listed with its source in WEIGHT_SOURCES.md in the app's source repository.",
  "weightEntryTitle": "Body composition and cycle hormones",
  "weightEntrySubtitle": "How body fat, energy availability, insulin and thyroid function interact with the cycle.",
  "movementTitle": "Pain and movement across the cycle",
  "movementIntro": "Reference notes on period pain, on what movement does and does not do about it, and on how much of the popular advice on the subject the evidence actually supports. This is background reading, not advice, and it describes what is known rather than any particular body. Nothing on this page is drawn from anything logged in this app.",
  "@movementIntro": {"description": "The framing sentence for the whole screen. It has to establish that the page is reference text about a subject, not addressed to the reader and not a plan, before anything else is named."},
  "movementNothingToFollowBody": "This app records no exercise and asks for none. There is nothing to enter here and no plan on this page to follow, so there is nothing on it to keep up with or to fall behind.",
  "@movementNothingToFollowBody": {"description": "Sits directly under the intro. The reader most likely to be harmed by this subject is the one who arrives expecting to be told what to do, and the first thing the section says is that it is not going to."},
  "movementPainTitle": "What period pain is",
  "movementPainBody": "Painful menstruation with no underlying disorder behind it is called primary dysmenorrhoea. The pain is typically cramping, felt low in the abdomen, strongest around the start of bleeding and easing over the following days. Prostaglandins released from the lining of the uterus drive the contractions that produce it. Where there is an underlying cause, such as endometriosis, adenomyosis or uterine fibroids, the term is secondary dysmenorrhoea.",
  "movementNotAvoidedTitle": "A period is not a reason to stop moving",
  "movementNotAvoidedBody": "Menstruation is not named anywhere in the sources behind this page as a reason to stop, and general physical activity guidance makes no exception for the days of a period. Clinical guidance on period pain goes further than that and lists exercise among the measures that may help with it.",
  "@movementNotAvoidedBody": {"description": "The correction this section exists for. The popular version of this subject says the opposite, and a cycle app repeating it would be telling people to stop doing something that helps them."},
  "movementEvidenceBody": "A Cochrane review of exercise for period pain found that exercise may reduce how intense the pain is, on evidence the reviewers themselves rated as low quality. That is a modest finding and it is stated here at its real strength rather than at a more useful one. What it supports is only this: the direction of the evidence runs towards movement helping with cramps rather than making them worse.",
  "@movementEvidenceBody": {"description": "Must not be strengthened. \"Low quality evidence that exercise may reduce pain\" is weaker than \"exercise helps period pain\" and it is the true one."},
  "movementHowItFeelsTitle": "What changes is how it feels",
  "movementHowItFeelsBody": "What varies across a cycle is the experience of moving, not what is permitted. Cramping pain around the start of bleeding, tiredness and low energy in the days before and during it, and how hard a given effort seems on the day are all part of that. The variation is wide between people and between one cycle and the next in the same person, which is why none of it turns into a rule.",
  "movementRestTitle": "Doing less is allowed",
  "movementRestBody": "Doing less, or nothing at all, on a day when that is what is wanted is a reasonable choice and not a lapse. That is a permission rather than an instruction, and the difference is the whole point: an instruction to rest is the same mistake as an instruction to push, only kinder. Nothing on this page asks for more and nothing on it asks for less.",
  "@movementRestBody": {"description": "Permission, never instruction. The requested version of this section was an instruction to rest, which is the same category error as an exercise prescription and is stated here as one."},
  "movementStopSignalsTitle": "Signals to stop",
  "movementStopSignalsBody": "Some things are reasons to stop rather than to carry on. Feeling faint or light-headed during exertion, breathlessness out of proportion to the effort, and chest pain are among them, and each of those is a reason to get it looked at rather than to push on. None of this is particular to menstruation; it holds whenever it happens.",
  "movementAnaemiaBody": "There is also a recognised reason why exertion can feel harder around heavy periods, and it is not that a cycle makes movement unwise. Heavy menstrual bleeding is a recognised cause of iron deficiency anaemia, whose commonly listed symptoms include tiredness, lack of energy and shortness of breath. Iron deficiency is confirmed by a blood test rather than by how a day felt, and it is worth taking to a doctor or nurse rather than working around.",
  "@movementAnaemiaBody": {"description": "The same claim the iron section makes, cited to the same sources in MOVEMENT_SOURCES.md and NUTRITION_SOURCES.md so the two cannot drift. It is here because it explains a hard day without blaming the cycle for it."},
  "movementPhaseClaimsTitle": "Planning exercise around cycle phase",
  "movementPhaseClaimsBody": "Schemes that assign different kinds of exercise to different parts of the cycle are widely promoted. The evidence underneath them is weak. Systematic review of cycle phase and exercise performance describes that research as low quality and inconsistent, with average effects that are trivial and with differences between individuals larger than the effect being looked for. No clinical guidance in the sources behind this page recommends planning exercise around cycle phase, and none says the chance of injury rises or falls with it.",
  "@movementPhaseClaimsBody": {"description": "Says outright that the evidence is weak rather than quietly declining to use it. A reference that handed someone a prescription built on evidence this thin would be worse than one that says how thin it is."},
  "movementContraceptionBody": "Hormonal contraception is a further reason those schemes do not carry across. Combined hormonal contraception works by suppressing ovulation, so the rise and fall of oestrogen and progesterone that a phase-based scheme assumes is not what is going on underneath. A scheme built on phases, applied where those phases are not occurring, has nothing left to stand on.",
  "movementSeeSomeoneTitle": "Pain worth taking to someone",
  "movementSeeSomeoneBody": "Period pain that is severe, that is getting worse, that the usual measures do not ease, or that gets in the way of ordinary days is a reason to see a doctor or nurse. So is pain that began later in life, changed in character, or arrives alongside other symptoms. The causes named above are found by assessment rather than by tracking dates. No threshold is set out here and no scale.",
  "@movementSeeSomeoneBody": {"description": "The last thing on the screen, pointing at a person. Deliberately carries no threshold and no pain scale: both are numbers, and a number here is something to be talked out of by."},
  "movementSourcesNote": "Every claim on this page is listed with its source in MOVEMENT_SOURCES.md in the app's source repository.",
  "movementEntryTitle": "Pain and movement across the cycle",
  "movementEntrySubtitle": "What exercise does about period pain, how little supports planning it by cycle phase, and what is a reason to stop.",

  "companionLabel": "Companion",
  "@companionLabel": {"description": "The name of the small persistent control in the bottom bar. It is a control, so it needs a name a screen reader can announce; 'decoration' is not an option."},
  "@companionHowItWorks": {"description": "Shown under every suggestion. States what the thing is: a rules engine over local data with a finite vocabulary, which knows what was logged and not how the reader feels."},
  "companionDismiss": "Dismiss",
  "@companionDismiss": {"description": "Closes the suggestion. The suggestion has to be dismissible from inside it, not only by the sheet's own gesture."},
  "companionSourcesNote": "Any health claim above is listed with its source in COMPANION_SOURCES.md in the app's source repository.",
  "@companionSourcesNote": {"description": "Points at the sources file. Some lines make no health claim at all, and the file says which ones and why."},

  "companionLineStillReading": "The log is still being read.",
  "@companionLineStillReading": {"description": "Shown while the logs, prediction or conditions are still loading. Says so rather than picking a line from a state it has not seen yet."},
  "companionLinePredictionPaused": "A pregnancy is recorded, so predictions are paused. Days can still be logged.",
  "@companionLinePredictionPaused": {"description": "Restates a state the user entered themselves and a behaviour the app already has. It never infers a pregnancy; it reads a recorded condition."},
  "companionLineHeavyBleeding": "Heavy bleeding is recorded on several days. Heavy menstrual bleeding is a recognised cause of iron deficiency anaemia, which a blood test settles rather than symptoms.",
  "@companionLineHeavyBleeding": {"description": "Carries a health claim (COMPANION_SOURCES.md I1, I2). A statement about physiology, followed by the sentence that stops it being read as a conclusion about the reader."},
  "companionLineCramps": "Cramps are recorded for today. Guidance on period pain lists heat, painkillers and exercise among the measures that may help.",
  "@companionLineCramps": {"description": "Carries a health claim (COMPANION_SOURCES.md P1). Reports what guidance lists rather than telling anyone to do any of it."},
  "companionLineSleep": "Disturbed sleep is recorded for today. Sleep problems are among the symptoms commonly reported around a period.",
  "@companionLineSleep": {"description": "Carries a health claim (COMPANION_SOURCES.md S1). Says the symptom is commonly reported, which is not the same as saying it is caused by the cycle."},
  "companionLineLowEnergy": "Low energy is recorded for today. Tiredness is among the symptoms commonly reported before and during a period.",
  "@companionLineLowEnergy": {"description": "Carries a health claim (COMPANION_SOURCES.md S2). Same shape as the sleep line and the same limit on it."},
  "companionLinePeriodToday": "A period is logged for today. Menstruation is not named anywhere in the guidance behind this app as a reason to stop moving.",
  "@companionLinePeriodToday": {"description": "Carries a health claim (COMPANION_SOURCES.md M1), an absence claim borrowed from the movement reference. It tells nobody to move; it says no source tells anyone to stop."},
  "companionLineOverdue": "The predicted window has passed with no period logged. That is a data state, and nothing here guesses at what is behind it.",
  "@companionLineOverdue": {"description": "The line PLAN 6.3 is most specific about. A missed period is a data state, not a life event to comment on, and the second sentence is what keeps it one."},
  "companionLineNothingLoggedRecently": "Nothing has been logged for a few days. The log is on the first tab, whenever there is something to put in it.",
  "@companionLineNothingLoggedRecently": {"description": "An offer, never a nudge. No streak, no count, and no reason asked for: a gap may be pregnancy, loss, illness, or simply not wanting to think about it."},
  "companionLineNoLogsYet": "Nothing is recorded yet. One period start date is enough to begin.",
  "@companionLineNoLogsYet": {"description": "The empty state, restating what onboarding already said rather than inventing a second version of it."},
  "companionLineNoPredictionYet": "There is not enough history for a prediction yet. Insights holds what has been recorded so far.",
  "@companionLineNoPredictionYet": {"description": "Says what is missing and where the user's own figures are, because withholding a prediction must not read as withholding their history."},
  "companionLineTodayLogged": "Today is already logged. The day can be opened again and changed.",
  "@companionLineTodayLogged": {"description": "Deliberately no praise. Logging is not an achievement and this app does not congratulate anyone for it."},
  "companionLineNothingToPointAt": "Nothing to point at just now.",
  "@companionLineNothingToPointAt": {"description": "The fallback. It says nothing rather than manufacturing something, which is the whole reason the set is finite."},

